<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astrakhan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xacitarxan, Hajji Tarkhan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Astrakhan is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> delta near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Its predecessor, Hajji Tarkhan or Xacitarxan, was a trading town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and from the 15th century the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> conquered the khanate in 1556, and the city, rebuilt on a nearby site with a stone kremlin, became Russia's gateway to the Caspian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astrakhan.jpg" alt="Astrakhan" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ic.pics.livejournal.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astrakhan_-_Russia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its successor states, the khanates of Kazan, Crimea, <b>Astrakhan</b> and Sibir, carried its political traditions into the early modern period, the Crimean Khanate surviving until 1783, and the Volga and Crimean Tatars, the Kazakhs and the Uzbeks all trace parts of their history to the Ulus of Jochi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His reign brought the conquest of Kazan and <b>Astrakhan</b> as well as the terror of the oprichnina.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-16">16 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Astrakhan Khanate formed in the 1460s around the town of <b>Xacitarxan</b> near the Volga delta, under khans descended from Küchük Muhammad of the Great Horde.</blockquote><a href="/state/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its remnants retreated towards <b>Astrakhan</b>, and Soviet rule returned to the North Caucasus only in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-caucasian-soviet-republic">North Caucasian Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The host traditionally dates its founding to 1577, when Russian Cossacks were recorded on the Terek after Muscovy's push down the Volga to <b>Astrakhan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-cossack-host">Terek Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
